# BUGBOT — Layered PR Review Posture

> Instructions for AI-assisted PR reviewers (Bugbot, Copilot for PRs, custom review bots). Copy to `.cursor/BUGBOT.md` and adapt to your architecture.

Each layer gets a different review posture. Reviewers (human or AI) should:

1. Identify the primary layer of the PR from `Layer Scope` in the PR description.
2. Load the matching posture below.
3. Apply the checks specific to that layer.

---

## Universal checks (all layers)

- [ ] PR follows the template (`.github/pull_request_template.md`).
- [ ] PR links to a ticket and an epic.
- [ ] PR size within ~400 impl lines OR has a stack rationale.
- [ ] All CI checks green (test, lint, typecheck).
- [ ] Layer scope declared and matches touched files.
- [ ] Acceptance Criteria explicitly mapped in the PR description.
- [ ] Change Log entry appended to affected specs (if divergence).

---

## Domain layer

- [ ] No framework imports (no React, no Express, no AWS SDK, no DB clients).
- [ ] Types match the Technical Solution Design.
- [ ] Business rules are pure functions (no side effects).
- [ ] Validation is present at every entry point.
- [ ] Constants are extracted into dedicated files.
- [ ] 100% test coverage on business rules (they're pure — no excuse).

## Data-access layer

- [ ] Repository interface defined + implementation is separate.
- [ ] All queries pageable when the dataset can grow.
- [ ] No `SELECT *` (or DynamoDB `SCAN` without filter + pagination).
- [ ] Idempotent writes where semantics allow.
- [ ] Timeouts / retries configured.
- [ ] Contract tests against a local DB (or LocalStack for DynamoDB).

## Functions / handlers layer

- [ ] Handler is thin (≤ 50 lines).
- [ ] Input validation with a schema library.
- [ ] Typed errors thrown from services; mapped to HTTP codes centrally.
- [ ] No business logic in the handler.
- [ ] Observability: structured logs, request ID, timing.
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or auth bypass.

## Infrastructure layer

- [ ] IaC formatted (Terraform: `terraform fmt`, Pulumi: `prettier`, CDK: `cdk synth`).
- [ ] No wildcard IAM policies.
- [ ] Sensitive resources have deletion protection.
- [ ] Env vars documented in the affected module's AGENTS.md.
- [ ] Cost implication acknowledged in PR description if resource size changes.

## Feature (UI) layer

- [ ] Uses design system components (no re-invented `Button`, `Modal`, etc.).
- [ ] Design tokens used (no hex colors, no hardcoded spacing).
- [ ] Accessibility: ARIA labels, keyboard nav, focus visible.
- [ ] Error states rendered (see `surface-all-errors.mdc`).
- [ ] Loading + empty states present.
- [ ] No inline JSX handlers with block bodies (see `no-inline-jsx-handlers.mdc`).
- [ ] Component under ~200 lines; decomposed if exceeds.
- [ ] Types in dedicated files if exported.
- [ ] Component tests using Testing Library (queries by role, not by test-id when possible).

## Experience (dev shell) layer

- [ ] Disposable — no durable product logic added here.
- [ ] Mock API stubs match the real API contract.
- [ ] Not deployed to production.

---

## Red flags — reject the PR

- Non-primary-layer edits without justification in PR description.
- Test file changes without a corresponding source file change (or vice versa).
- New dependencies added without an ADR or rationale.
- Auth / authz logic in a UI component (should be in `functions/`).
- Business logic in a `handler` (should be in `services/`).
- `error.message.includes(...)` patterns.
- `JSON.parse(...) as Type` patterns.
- Hex color literals in style props.
- New AGENTS.md-level convention discussed in PR review not documented.

---

## Escalation

If the PR touches multiple layers or introduces a new pattern:

- Reviewer should tag the tech lead.
- Reviewer should ask "should this be an ADR?"
- Do not merge without tech lead sign-off.
